sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean: graded-beam curvature integral reducing to Timoshenko" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling> ;
    dct:description "Generalize the existing Hygromorphs.lean results from two uniform layers to arbitrary through-thickness profiles E(z) and eigenstrain eps(z). With moments A = integral E, B = integral E z, D = integral E z^2, P = integral E eps, Q = integral E z eps over the thickness, force and moment balance give curvature kappa = (A Q - B P)/(A D - B^2). Prove this from equilibrium for integrable profiles (interval integrals in Mathlib), prove A D - B^2 > 0 for positive non-constant-free E (Cauchy-Schwarz), and prove it reduces to the existing bilayerCurvature for piecewise-constant two-layer profiles. Also prove that for a two-layer strip with diffusivities D1 and D2 the slowest diffusion time lies between h^2/(pi^2 D_max) and h^2/(pi^2 D_min) for the stated separable model, or state the bound as a conditional theorem if the spectral argument exceeds current Mathlib." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Compiled Lean theorems for the graded curvature formula and its reduction to Timoshenko, with no sorry." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Integration lemmas for piecewise-constant functions too cumbersome to close the reduction; diffusion bracket left unproved (partial completion)." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the moment integrals with intervalIntegral and state equilibrium. 2. Prove the curvature formula and the positivity of the denominator. 3. Prove the reduction to bilayerCurvature for two-layer step profiles. 4. Attempt the diffusion-time bracket; if not feasible, record precisely what Mathlib lacks. 5. Update the Hygromorphs report." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: The diffusion-time bracket for heterogeneous layers needs spectral theory of Sturm-Liouville operators that Mathlib may not provide." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mathlib" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch10-01> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1364/josa.11.000233> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0184> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Do real hygromorphs follow bilayer curvature and diffusive response-time scaling?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1364/josa.11.000233> dct:title "Analysis of Bi-Metal Thermostats" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Timoshenko 1925, Analysis of bi-metal thermostats" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0184> dct:title "Hygromorphs: from pine cones to biomimetic bilayers" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Reyssat and Mahadevan 2009" .
}

